In student assessment, acceptable use of GenAI will depend on the nature and purpose of the assessment.
Programmes are being updated to ensure that students have a mix of GenAI-secure and GenAI-open assessments.
All programmes will make use of a range of assessments clearly identified as either:
No GenAI Allowed (including some assessments where AI use is not possible, such as viva);
Selective, where it will be specified how GenAI may be used;
Allowed, where students may choose whether and how to use GenAI;
Embedded, where using GenAI is required.

Throughout the Conduct Process, the Respondent, as party to the proceedings, is granted the following rights:
to seek information from a Student Conduct Administrator and/or Investigator;
to be informed of the charge(s) and alleged misconduct upon which the charge is based;
to be informed of the Information upon which a charge is based and afforded an opportunity to offer a relevant response;
to use an Advisor;
to remain silent with no inference of responsibility drawn;
to call and question relevant Witnesses;
to present Information in his/her behalf;
to be considered not responsible until proven responsible by a Preponderance of the Evidence;
to be informed of the outcome of the disciplinary proceeding in writing;
to appeal the decision;
to waive any of the above rights; and
to have resolution of the case within a reasonable time.

Article 86. Penalty
§1. General
Artikel General
The examination committee independently assesses the severity of the irregularities and determines an appropriate penalty.
In cases of plagiarism, the severity and penalty are assessed based on the following elements:
1° scope;
2° nature;
3° experience level of the students involved (the extent to which students are expected to be aware of the importance and implications of academic integrity standards, taking into account, inter alia, the students' position in their student career).
When deciding on a penalty for an irregularity, the intention to commit fraud can be taken into account as an aggravating factor. If the committee considers a penalty to be of limited impact on the students' curriculum, it can make a substantiated decision to impose a more severe penalty.​​
§2. Penalties
Artikel General
On the grounds of an irregularity committed during the examination, the examination committee may, without prejudice to articles 89 §2 and 90 §2, decide to establish the irregularity during the examination board meeting or an early meeting without imposing a penalty. Alternatively, the students in question may receive one of the following penalties or a combination of penalties 1 to 5:
1° have taken an invalid examination and have to retake the examination at a time and date set by the faculty;
2° will receive an adjusted mark for the examination of the course or part of the course;
3° will receive a 0 on the examination of the course or a part of the course;
4° will receive a 0 for some or all of the courses for which they took an examination during the examination period in question;

